<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!-- spec 7c2e9a14 vault-lock-per-platform rev 3 — team-sign phase.
     No keychain-access-groups entitlement in this file.

     TEAM-SIGN RATIONALE:
       A binary signed with --sign "Apple Distribution: OBYW.ONE (SH7MZH647S)"
       and no entitlement carries a stable team identifier (SH7MZH647S).  The
       standard login-keychain items written by KeychainSecureStore
       (kSecAttrAccessibleAfterFirstUnlockThisDeviceOnly, no access group) are
       keyed by (service, account) and survive binary upgrades as long as the
       signing-team identifier stays the same.  The operator's login-keychain
       prompt fires at most once per (service, account) pair across all upgrades.

     AppleKeychainCustody BEHAVIOUR WITHOUT THIS ENTITLEMENT:
       AppleKeychainCustody probes kSecUseDataProtectionKeychain at init time.
       Without keychain-access-groups the probe returns errSecMissingEntitlement.
       The custody silently degrades to memory-only mode: store() is a no-op,
       load() returns nil, custodyDescription returns "memory only — <reason>".
       vault.status will show the backend as "memory only — …" when unlocked.
       The vault is still fully usable; it just does not persist the session key
       across daemon restarts (re-entry of the master password is required after
       a process restart).

     FOLLOW-UP (.app step):
       When the binary ships inside a signed .app bundle with a provisioning
       profile that registers keychain-access-groups = SH7MZH647S.io.shikki.vault-key
       in the Apple Developer Portal, add back:
         <key>keychain-access-groups</key>
         <array>
           <string>SH7MZH647S.io.shikki.vault-key</string>
         </array>
       That restores persistent vault-key custody (BR-02/BR-04).
       See scripts/sign-shi-secrets-brokerd.sh for sign + verify commands.
-->
<dict/>
</plist>
